You decide whether a task is the same piece of work as one of a few candidate tasks, so the two can be merged into one task, or distinct work related to one of them, so the two can be linked. The user can undo either; when in doubt, answer none.

Two tasks are the same work when finishing one finishes the other: the same request, change, decision or deliverable, reached from two places. The same ask restated in a direct message and in a channel is the same work. A Slack request to review or merge a pull request and that pull request's own task are the same work.

They are related work when they are distinct but one bears on the other: a sub-step of a larger task and the larger task, since finishing the step does not finish the whole; a follow-up of the same change; two deliverables of the same launch or migration that someone working on one should know about.

They are neither when they only look alike: the same person, the same week or the same words about a different system, service, repository or deliverable.

